[142.457203, "i", "cat >> README.md <<'MD'\n\n## Resume and durability\n\nEach checkpoint records the last committed document ID, the committed row count,\nand the last shard's byte offset and row count. The worker flushes and fsyncs all\nrows (including shards closed during rotation), syncs the index directory, then\nwrites/fsyncs a temporary checkpoint and atomically replaces the checkpoint file.\nThe checkpoint directory is synced after replacement. Source iteration resumes\nstrictly **after** the committed ID; IDs need not be contiguous.\n\nAt startup, the worker truncates the last shard to the committed offset and\nremoves later shards. This discards any uncommitted suffix, including a torn JSON\nline left by SIGKILL. Recovery is repeatable if interrupted. SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP\nrequest a cooperative stop: finish the current document and commit the partial\nbatch. A forced kill may replay uncommitted work, but never skips committed work.\n`./jobs status` counts only committed rows, not the speculative suffix.\n\nCheckpoints from the old, un"]
[142.457238, "i", "safe protocol have no durable boundary and trigger a\none-time rebuild. Malformed/unsupported new checkpoints or a truncated committed\nshard fail rather than silently discarding acknowledged progress. Do not edit a\ncheckpoint to advance it manually. The corpus must remain stable during a build;\nuse `./jobs clean` to start a new build after corpus changes. Normal supervised\nruns are serialized by the supervisor lock.\nMD\n"]
[142.457299, "o", "cat >> README.md <<'MD'"]
